<header><p class="st-kicker">kingdom · Horn and East Africa</p><h1 class="st-title">Limmu-Ennarea</h1><p class="st-meta">1800 CE – 1891 CE (approx.) · capital Saqqa</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">An Oromo kingdom of the Gibe region, built on the lands of old Ennarea, which flourished under King Abba Bagibo as a hub of the gold and ivory trade.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>In the 18th century <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oromo">Oromo</a> groups settled the former territory of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/kingdom-of-ennarea">Kingdom of Ennarea</a>. Around 1800 a monarchy arose there under Bofo, and his son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abba-bagibo">Abba Bagibo</a> (r. c. 1825 to 1861) turned <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/limmu">Limmu</a>-Ennarea into the most powerful of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gibe-river">Gibe</a> kingdoms, with its capital at Saqqa.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The king ruled through appointed officials and controlled the great market at Saqqa, where traders from the north exchanged cloth, salt and metal goods for gold, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ivory-trade">ivory</a>, civet, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/coffee-economy">coffee</a> and slaves. The dynasty adopted <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/islam">Islam</a>, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abba-bagibo">Abba Bagibo</a> married into the royal families of neighbouring states. Muslim merchants, the jabarti, played a large role in its economy.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>After <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abba-bagibo">Abba Bagibo's</a> death the kingdom declined in wars with the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/kingdom-of-jimma">Kingdom of Jimma</a> and others. It was conquered by the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/ethiopian-empire">Ethiopian Empire</a> under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/menelik-ii">Menelik II</a> in 1891.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/limmu-ennarea.jpg" alt="Limmu-Ennarea" width="320" height="232"><figcaption>Image: Antoine d'Abbadie · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Antoine_d%27Abbadie_-_Vue_10_-_Fronti%C3%A8re_septentrionale_du_Kaffa_b_(cropped).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1800 CE – 1891 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Century</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>kingdom</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Saqqa</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Abba Bagibo</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Sunni Islam</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Oromo</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/ethiopia">Ethiopia</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Kingdom of Limmu-Ennarea, Limmu</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1846&amp;focus=limmu-ennarea" aria-label="Limmu-Ennarea on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5701 1871 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Limmu-Ennarea lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M6021 2127L6029 1964"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M6029 1964h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M6021 2127h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M6021 2127h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks its recorded location. <a href="/atlas?year=1846&amp;focus=limmu-ennarea">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
